From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Robert Jefe Lindstaedt <robert.lindstaedt@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 00:13:24 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] metal: build render pipeline states off the render thread for
 async-compiled programs
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

With asynchronous shader compilation on, the library compile leaves the
render thread, but the first draw of each program still builds its render
pipeline state synchronously with newRenderPipelineState. That is where
Metal compiles the program for the GPU, and on a cold shader cache it
blocks the render thread (the main thread on iOS) for longer than the
library compile did: an iOS app reported "App hanging for at least
2000 ms" inside ShaderProgram::getRenderPipelineState →
AGX::Compiler::compileProgram on the first launch of a fresh install,
with the library compile already taken off the main thread.

A program compiled asynchronously now builds its pipeline states
asynchronously too, with the completion-handler form of
newRenderPipelineState, keyed by the same reuse hash as the pipeline
state cache. getRenderPipelineState returns null while the state is
building; the drawable skips its draw for that frame
(ShaderProgram::isPipelineStatePending) instead of asserting; the build
is counted with the pending library compiles, so the renderer keeps
requesting frames until it lands, and the state then enters the cache as
before. A failed asynchronous build falls back to one synchronous attempt,
which logs and asserts as before. Synchronously compiled programs (the
clipping mask, and every program while the option is off) are unchanged.
